// ================================================================
// OMNI SCHEMA CANONS - The One Universal Schema
// ================================================================

Canon {
  name = comp_project
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Top-level container linking domain, model, strategy, and hardware for a complete system definition."
  doc = ~~~
Project is the root organizational unit in Omni:

**Purpose:**
Binds together all aspects of a system:
- Domain: Knowledge area (daml, net5, nexus, tsu, custom)
- Model: The compute graph/architecture
- Strategy: Optimization approach
- Hardware: Target execution substrate

**Design:**
Project is intentionally minimal - just enough to link the key components.
Detailed configuration lives in referenced components, not in Project itself.

**Usage Pattern:**
```rio
Project {
  project = MaxCutOptimizer
  domain = tsu
  model = maxcut_qubo_model
  strategy = simulated_annealing
  hardware = tsu_extropic_v1
  desc = "Max-Cut optimization using thermodynamic sampling"
}
```

All other components reference back through these root links.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_domain
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Knowledge domain tag organizing projects by area: daml, net5, nexus, tsu, quantum, neuromorphic, etc."
  doc = ~~~
Domain categorizes projects by knowledge area:

**Standard Domains:**
- daml: Deep Adaptive Machine Learning
- net5: Universal neural architecture framework
- nexus: Kernel fusion and optimization
- tsu: Thermodynamic Sampling Unit
- quantum: Quantum computing
- photonic: Optical computing
- neuromorphic: Brain-inspired computing
- molecular: Molecular computing

**Purpose:**
Domains enable:
- Organization by field
- Domain-specific tooling
- Cross-domain comparison
- Community standards

**Extensibility:**
Create custom domains for proprietary or emerging paradigms.
Domain is just a tag - the real structure is in Model, Op, Hardware.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_hardware
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Universal execution substrate definition supporting GPUs, TPUs, TSUs, QPUs, photonic chips, analog arrays, and future architectures."
  doc = ~~~
Hardware represents any execution substrate:

**Classical:**
- gpu_a100: NVIDIA A100 GPU
- tpu_v4: Google TPU v4
- cpu_epyc: AMD EPYC CPU

**Thermodynamic:**
- tsu_extropic_v1: Extropic TSU
- tsu_simulator: Software simulation

**Quantum:**
- ibmq_oslo: IBM Quantum Oslo
- rigetti_aspen: Rigetti Aspen-M-3

**Emerging:**
- photonic_lightmatter: Lightmatter photonic chip
- analog_mythic: Mythic analog array
- neuromorphic_loihi: Intel Loihi 2

**Features:**
- Hierarchical: parent_hw for rack→node→card
- Emulation: Reference software emulator
- Noise: Link to noise model
- Payload: Architecture details in JSON/YAML

**Design Philosophy:**
Hardware is a first-class entity. Operations target specific hardware.
No assumptions about von Neumann architecture.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_model
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "The compute graph/neural architecture/circuit - contains Layers, Tensors, Ops that define the computation."
  doc = ~~~
Model is the computational structure:

**What It Represents:**
- Neural network architecture
- Probabilistic graphical model
- Quantum circuit
- Analog circuit
- Hybrid computation graph

**Structure:**
Model contains:
- Layer: Hierarchical organization (blocks, cells, experts)
- Tensor: Data carriers (activations, weights, qubits, voltages)
- Op: Operations transforming tensors

**Design:**
- Models are hardware-agnostic by default
- Hardware preferences expressed via references
- Can reference SearchSpace for NAS
- Can reference Config for runtime settings
- Optional code field for main driver

**Payload:**
Type, dataset, num_classes, architecture details in JSON/YAML.

**Philosophy:**
Model is pure structure. Hardware and Strategy are separate concerns.
Same Model can target multiple Hardware substrates.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_layer
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Hierarchical grouping of operations: cells, blocks, experts, circuit sections - supports nested organization."
  doc = ~~~
Layer provides hierarchical organization within Models:

**Use Cases:**
- Neural network layers (conv1, fc2, attention_block)
- NAS cells (normal_cell, reduction_cell)
- MoE experts (expert_0, expert_1)
- Quantum circuit sections (initialization, entanglement, measurement)

**Nesting:**
parent_layer enables arbitrary depth:
```
Layer backbone
  Layer stage_1
    Layer block_1
    Layer block_2
  Layer stage_2
    Layer block_3
```

**Design:**
Layers are organizational only - they don't execute.
Operations (Op) do the work; Layers group them logically.

**Payload:**
layer_type, repeat count, reduction flags, etc.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_block
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "NET5 universal reusable block template - references a sub-Model for structure reuse across architectures."
  doc = ~~~
Block enables modular, reusable architecture components:

**Purpose:**
Define once, instantiate many times:
- ResNet block
- Transformer block
- Mixture-of-experts expert
- Quantum error correction block

**Design:**
Block references a Model for its internal structure.
When instantiated, the Model's Layers/Tensors/Ops are instantiated.

**Payload:**
block_type, parameters, noise_model, energy budget, etc.

**Pattern:**
```rio
Model {
  model = resnet_block
}

Block {
  block = basic_resnet_block
  model = resnet_block
}

Model {
  model = resnet50
}

Layer {
  layer = stage_1
  // Instantiates basic_resnet_block multiple times
}
```

NET5 pattern: composition from universal blocks.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_tensor
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Universal data carrier: classical tensors, spiking events, qubit states, analog voltages - anything that flows through the computation."
  doc = ~~~
Tensor represents any data in the system:

**Classical:**
- Activations: input, hidden_0, output
- Weights: conv1_weights, attention_qkv
- Gradients: hidden_0_grad

**Quantum:**
- Qubit states: qubits_0, ancilla
- Measurement results: measurement_bitstring

**Thermodynamic:**
- P-bit states: pbit_array
- Energy values: qubo_matrix

**Neuromorphic:**
- Spike trains: spike_events_layer_1
- Membrane potentials: vmem_neurons

**Photonic/Analog:**
- Optical signals: optical_input_waveguide
- Voltage levels: analog_crossbar_row_0

**Fields:**
- producer: Op that creates this tensor
- distribution: EnergyFunction for probabilistic semantics
- payload: shape, dtype, layout, symbolic shape, role

**Philosophy:**
Tensor is type-agnostic. Payload describes specifics.
Same abstraction spans all computing paradigms.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_op
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "The One True Universal Operation - replaces 70+ legacy subtypes, spans all computing paradigms from classical to quantum to thermodynamic."
  doc = ~~~
Op is the universal computation primitive:

**Design Philosophy:**
One Op type for everything:
- Classical: MatMul, Conv2D, ReLU, Softmax
- Quantum: CNOT, Hadamard, Measurement, StatePrep
- Thermodynamic: GibbsSample, QUBOUpdate, AnnealingSchedule
- Neuromorphic: SpikeTrain, LIF, STDP
- Photonic: WavelengthMux, PhaseShift, Interferometer
- Analog: DotProduct, ThresholdActivation

**Structure:**
- hardware: Target substrate
- energy_fn: Physics-based energy (optional)
- search_space: NAS context
- strategy: Meta-optimization approach
- predicate: Dynamic condition tensor
- next_op: Explicit DAG edge
- layer: Containing layer
- code: File path to implementation (ALWAYS a path, never inline)
- payload: JSON/YAML with op-specific details

**Key Innovation:**
No op_type enumeration. Type is in payload.
Same schema handles operations we haven't invented yet.

**Code as Path:**
```rio
Op {
  op = qubo_gibbs_update
  code = kernels/qubo_gibbs.cu
  payload = """
  {
    "op_type": "GibbsSampling",
    "temperature": 300.0,
    "num_sweeps": 1000
  }
  """
}
```

Never inline code. Always reference files.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_arg
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Input/output/parameter binding connecting Ops to Tensors with explicit roles."
  doc = ~~~
Arg connects operations to data:

**Roles:**
- input: Data consumed by Op
- output: Data produced by Op
- parameter: Learnable weights

**Reference Pattern:**
```
Element model  up_copy   Model
Element tensor ref_child Tensor
```

This is the canonical ref_child pattern:
1. up_copy walks up to parent Model
2. ref_child looks up sibling Tensor within that Model

**Example:**
```rio
Op {
  op = matmul_1
}

Arg {
  arg = input_a
  role = input
  tensor = hidden_0
}

Arg {
  arg = input_b
  role = input
  tensor = weights_fc1
}

Arg {
  arg = output_c
  role = output
  tensor = hidden_1
}
```

Explicit argument binding makes dataflow clear.
Supports multi-input/multi-output operations.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_config
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Runtime and inference configuration: batch size, precision, execution schedule, state transfer settings."
  doc = ~~~
Config specifies how to run a Model:

**Purpose:**
Separate structure (Model) from execution parameters (Config).
Same Model can have multiple Configs for different scenarios.

**Common Settings:**
- batch_size: Batch size for inference/training
- precision: fp32, fp16, int8, mixed
- state_transfer: classical↔quantum state handling
- schedule: Execution strategy reference

**Pattern:**
```rio
Config {
  config = inference_optimized
  schedule = batch_schedule_strategy
  payload = """
  {
    "batch_size": 128,
    "precision": "fp16",
    "cache_size_mb": 256
  }
  """
}

Model {
  model = my_model
  config = inference_optimized
}
```

Configs are shareable across Models.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_kernel
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Legacy component for backward compatibility - new designs use Op.code directly instead."
  doc = ~~~
Kernel is deprecated in favor of Op.code:

**Old Pattern (Don't Use):**
```rio
Kernel {
  kernel = my_kernel
  hardware = gpu_a100
  code = kernels/my_kernel.cu
}

Op {
  op = my_op
  kernel = my_kernel
}
```

**New Pattern (Use This):**
```rio
Op {
  op = my_op
  hardware = gpu_a100
  code = kernels/my_kernel.cu
}
```

**Why Deprecated:**
Kernel was redundant with Op.code.
Simplified schema: one less component type.

**When to Use:**
Only when maintaining legacy systems.
All new designs should use Op.code directly.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_energyfunction
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Energy landscape definition for thermodynamic, annealing, and quantum operations - defines the optimization surface."
  doc = ~~~
EnergyFunction defines physics-based energy landscapes:

**Use Cases:**
- Thermodynamic sampling: Boltzmann distributions
- Quantum annealing: Ising/QUBO Hamiltonians
- Simulated annealing: Cost functions
- Probabilistic inference: Energy-based models

**Structure:**
- code: Optional source file for energy calculation
- payload: Energy expression, variables, temperature, etc.

**Example:**
```rio
EnergyFunction {
  energy_fn = maxcut_qubo
  payload = """
  {
    "expression": "sum(Q[i,j] * s[i] * s[j])",
    "variables": ["s"],
    "temperature": 300.0,
    "units": "eV"
  }
  """
}

Op {
  op = sample_step
  energy_fn = maxcut_qubo
}
```

**Philosophy:**
Energy is first-class. Not all computation is deterministic.
Thermodynamic and quantum systems are energy-driven.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_searchspace
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "NAS, architecture search, and hyperparameter tuning space definition with Dimensions for each tunable parameter."
  doc = ~~~
SearchSpace defines what can be optimized:

**Structure:**
- target_model: Model being tuned
- Dimension children: Individual tunable parameters
- payload: Algorithm hints, constraints

**Example:**
```rio
SearchSpace {
  space = nas_search
  target_model = my_model
}

Dimension {
  dimension = num_layers
  payload = """{"type": "int", "range": [1, 20]}"""
}

Dimension {
  dimension = layer_width
  payload = """{"type": "int", "range": [32, 512], "scale": "log"}"""
}

Dimension {
  dimension = activation
  payload = """{"type": "categorical", "choices": ["relu", "gelu", "swish"]}"""
}
```

**Dimension Types:**
- Continuous: Temperature, learning rate
- Discrete: Number of layers, heads
- Categorical: Activation function, block type
- Conditional: Depend on other dimensions

SearchSpace + Strategy = automated optimization.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_dimension
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Single tunable parameter within a SearchSpace: continuous, discrete, categorical, or conditional."
  doc = ~~~
Dimension is one degree of freedom in search:

**Types:**
- Continuous: Real-valued range [min, max]
- Discrete: Integer range [min, max]
- Categorical: Fixed set of choices
- Conditional: Depends on other dimensions

**Payload:**
- type: continuous | discrete | categorical | conditional
- range: [min, max] for numeric
- choices: [opt1, opt2, ...] for categorical
- scale: linear | log | exp
- condition: Expression for conditional dimensions

**Examples:**
```rio
// Continuous
Dimension {
  dimension = learning_rate
  payload = """{"type": "continuous", "range": [1e-5, 1e-1], "scale": "log"}"""
}

// Discrete
Dimension {
  dimension = num_experts
  payload = """{"type": "discrete", "range": [4, 16]}"""
}

// Categorical
Dimension {
  dimension = optimizer
  payload = """{"type": "categorical", "choices": ["adam", "sgd", "rmsprop"]}"""
}
```

Dimensions compose to define high-dimensional search spaces.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_strategy
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Optimization, simulation, search, or execution strategy: DARTS, evolution, MAML, cycle-accurate simulation, etc."
  doc = ~~~
Strategy defines HOW to optimize or execute:

**Types:**
- NAS: DARTS, evolutionary search, reinforcement learning
- Meta-learning: MAML, Reptile, ProtoNets
- Simulation: Cycle-accurate, event-driven, continuous time
- Execution: Scheduling, mapping, resource allocation

**Structure:**
- search_space: What's being optimized
- fitness: Metric to optimize
- code: Implementation file path
- payload: Learning rates, budgets, fidelity levels

**Example:**
```rio
Strategy {
  strategy = darts_nas
  search_space = nas_search
  fitness = validation_accuracy
  code = strategies/darts.py
  payload = """
  {
    "learning_rate": 0.025,
    "arch_learning_rate": 3e-4,
    "epochs": 50
  }
  """
}
```

**Philosophy:**
Strategy is separate from Model and Hardware.
Same Model can be optimized with different Strategies.
Same Strategy can apply to different Models.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_constraint
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Hard or soft constraints on operations or hardware: power, memory, fidelity, determinism, timing."
  doc = ~~~
Constraint limits what's allowed:

**Types:**
- Power: Max wattage, energy budget
- Memory: Max buffer size, bandwidth
- Fidelity: Min accuracy, max error rate
- Timing: Latency, throughput requirements
- Determinism: Reproducibility needs
- Noise: Max noise level for quantum/analog

**Structure:**
- target_hw: Constrained hardware
- model: Parent model context
- target_op: Specific operation constrained
- payload: Condition, severity, target value, noise model

**Example:**
```rio
Constraint {
  constraint_id = power_budget
  target_hw = tsu_extropic_v1
  payload = """
  {
    "constraint_type": "power",
    "max_watts": 50,
    "severity": "hard"
  }
  """
}

Constraint {
  constraint_id = memory_limit
  target_op = large_matmul
  payload = """
  {
    "constraint_type": "memory",
    "max_bytes": 1073741824,
    "severity": "soft"
  }
  """
}
```

Constraints guide optimization and scheduling.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_metric
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Telemetry and fitness metrics: accuracy, latency, energy, throughput, fidelity - anything measurable."
  doc = ~~~
Metric defines what to measure:

**Categories:**
- Quality: Accuracy, F1, perplexity
- Performance: Latency, throughput, FLOPS
- Efficiency: Energy per inference, power
- Hardware: Temperature, utilization
- Quantum: Fidelity, coherence time
- Thermodynamic: Convergence rate, energy gap

**Structure:**
- target_hw: What's being measured
- payload: Unit, target value, measurement method

**Example:**
```rio
Metric {
  metric = inference_latency
  target_hw = gpu_a100
  payload = """
  {
    "unit": "ms",
    "target_value": 10.0,
    "measurement": "p99"
  }
  """
}

Metric {
  metric = quantum_fidelity
  payload = """
  {
    "unit": "probability",
    "target_value": 0.99,
    "measurement": "state_overlap"
  }
  """
}
```

Metrics are objectives for Strategy optimization.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_checkpoint
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Versioned snapshot of model state: weights, optimizer state, training progress - enables resumption and rollback."
  doc = ~~~
Checkpoint preserves model state:

**Purpose:**
- Resume training after interruption
- Save best-performing states
- A/B test different checkpoints
- Rollback to previous versions

**Structure:**
- model: Model this snapshot belongs to
- payload: State file path, timestamp, metadata

**Example:**
```rio
Checkpoint {
  checkpoint_id = best_epoch_47
  model = my_model
  payload = """
  {
    "state_path": "checkpoints/model_epoch_47.pt",
    "timestamp": "2025-11-26T14:30:00Z",
    "epoch": 47,
    "val_accuracy": 0.947,
    "train_loss": 0.023
  }
  """
}
```

**Best Practices:**
- Regular checkpoints during training
- Save top-k by metric
- Version control checkpoint files
- Include metadata for traceability
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_fusion
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "NEXUS kernel fusion patterns for optimization: which operations fuse together on which hardware."
  doc = ~~~
Fusion optimizes by combining operations:

**Purpose:**
Reduce memory traffic and kernel launch overhead by fusing:
- Element-wise chains: ReLU → Add → ReLU
- Reduction patterns: Softmax, LayerNorm
- Producer-consumer: MatMul → Bias → Activation

**Structure:**
- hardware: Target hardware for fusion
- payload: Pattern type, operations fused, strategy

**Example:**
```rio
Fusion {
  fusion = conv_bn_relu
  hardware = gpu_a100
  payload = """
  {
    "pattern_type": "vertical",
    "ops_fused": ["Conv2D", "BatchNorm", "ReLU"],
    "strategy": "tensorcore",
    "speedup": 1.8
  }
  """
}
```

**Fusion Strategies:**
- Vertical: Producer-consumer chains
- Horizontal: Parallel operations
- Tiled: Loop fusion with tiling
- Multi-kernel: Cross-kernel optimization

NEXUS pattern: declarative fusion specifications.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = comp_controlflow
  category = omni_schema
  topic_type = component_definition
  overview = "Branching and looping logic within operations: if-then-else, loops, dynamic control based on tensor predicates."
  doc = ~~~
ControlFlow adds dynamic execution:

**Purpose:**
Enable conditional and iterative computation:
- If-then-else based on tensor values
- Loops with dynamic bounds
- Early exit conditions
- Dynamic routing (MoE gating)

**Structure:**
- predicate: Condition tensor (boolean)
- branch_true: Op to execute if true
- branch_false: Op to execute if false
- payload: Loop count, condition type

**Example:**
```rio
Op {
  op = main_computation
}

ControlFlow {
  control = dynamic_branch
  predicate = is_converged
  branch_true = finalize_op
  branch_false = continue_iteration
  payload = """
  {
    "condition_type": "early_exit",
    "max_iterations": 1000
  }
  """
}
```

**Use Cases:**
- Adaptive computation time
- Mixture of experts routing
- Iterative refinement
- Conditional processing

**Up_copy Pattern:**
Multiple up_copy fields navigate to Model for finding tensors/ops:
```
Element model1    up_copy Model
Element predicate ref_child Tensor
Element model2    up_copy Model
Element branch_true ref_child Op
```
~~~
}

// ================================================================
// RIO FORMAT CANONS
// ================================================================

Canon {
  name = rio_format_philosophy
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = design_philosophy
  overview = "Rio is a lightweight, human-writable format for hardware, models, and domains - readable, editable, auto-parsing JSON, safe code blocks."
  doc = ~~~
Rio Design Goals:

**Human-First:**
- Readable: Clear block structure
- Editable: Text editor friendly
- Commentable: // comments throughout

**Machine-Friendly:**
- Auto-parses JSON: "{"a":1}" → native types
- Type inference: 1024 → int, 300.0 → float, true → bool
- Structured data: """ """ for JSON, ~~~ ~~~ for code

**Domain-Agnostic:**
- Works for ML, quantum, thermodynamic, photonic
- No assumptions about field names
- Extensible to new domains

**Safe:**
- Code never inline in data
- Code blocks (~~~) preserve exactly
- No injection vulnerabilities

**Single File:**
Everything in one .rio file:
- Domain definitions
- Hardware specs
- Model architecture
- Checkpoints
- Metrics

**Pronunciation:** /ˈri.oʊ/ (like "Rio de Janeiro")
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_block_structure
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = syntax
  overview = "Rio files are sequences of typed blocks: BlockType { key-value pairs } with unique names and field assignments."
  doc = ~~~
Rio Block Syntax:

**Basic Structure:**
```rio
BlockType {
  field_name = value
  another_field = "string value"
  numeric_field = 42
}
```

**Block Components:**
- BlockType: Component type (Project, Model, Op, etc.)
- Block name: Optional, usually the first field (key type)
- Body: { ... } containing field assignments
- Fields: name = value pairs

**Example:**
```rio
Project {
  project = MyProject
  domain = tsu
  desc = "A thermodynamic optimizer"
}

Model {
  model = my_model
  hardware = tsu_v1
}
```

**Naming:**
- Quoted names: "name with spaces"
- Unquoted names: simple_name (no spaces)

**Field Values:**
Auto-parsed by type:
- Numbers: 42, 3.14, 1e-5
- Bools: true, false
- Strings: "text", unquoted_word
- Arrays: [item1, item2, item3]
- Objects: {key: value, key2: value2}
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_value_types
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = syntax
  overview = "Rio auto-detects types: int, float, bool, string, JSON arrays, JSON objects - or falls back to raw string."
  doc = ~~~
Rio Type Inference:

**Automatic Detection:**
Parser attempts JSON unmarshaling for every value.
If successful → native type; if fails → string.

**Examples:**
```rio
Model {
  // Integer
  num_layers = 12

  // Float
  temperature = 300.0
  learning_rate = 1e-4

  // Boolean
  training = true
  frozen = false

  // String (unquoted)
  name = my_model

  // String (quoted)
  description = "A model with spaces"

  // Array
  shape = [224, 224, 3]
  devices = [gpu0, gpu1, gpu2]

  // Object
  config = {batch_size: 32, precision: "fp16"}

  // Complex nested
  hyperparams = {
    optimizer: "adam",
    lr: 0.001,
    betas: [0.9, 0.999]
  }
}
```

**Fallback:**
If JSON parsing fails, value stored as raw string.
Enables forwards compatibility with unknown formats.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_multiline_json
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = syntax
  overview = "Triple-quote \"\"\" blocks for structured JSON that spans multiple lines - auto-parsed into native types."
  doc = ~~~
Structured Multi-line with """ """:

**Purpose:**
Complex JSON/YAML data that needs readability:

**Syntax:**
```rio
Model {
  model = complex_model
  payload = """
  {
    "architecture": "transformer",
    "num_layers": 12,
    "hidden_size": 768,
    "num_heads": 12,
    "vocab_size": 50257,
    "max_position_embeddings": 1024,
    "layer_norm_epsilon": 1e-5,
    "dropout": 0.1
  }
  """
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Content between """ """ → JSON parse attempt
- Success → native map[string]any
- Failure → raw string

**Closing Delimiter Rules:**
- Can be indented
- Must be alone on line (after whitespace trim)
- No content after """ on same line

**Use Cases:**
- Configuration objects
- Metric definitions
- Hardware specifications
- Checkpoint metadata
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_multiline_code
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = syntax
  overview = "Triple-tilde ~~~ blocks for verbatim code preservation - CUDA, Python, shaders - never parsed, always exact text."
  doc = ~~~
Verbatim Multi-line with ~~~ ~~~:

**Purpose:**
Preserve code exactly: kernels, templates, documentation.

**Syntax:**
```rio
Op {
  op = custom_kernel
  code = ~~~
__global__ void my_kernel(float* data) {
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    printf("He said \"\"\"triple quotes work here\"\"\"");
    data[idx] = data[idx] * 2.0;
}
~~~
}
```

**Behavior:**
- Content between ~~~ ~~~ → NEVER parsed
- Always stored as raw string
- Preserves indentation, newlines, everything
- Can contain """, ''', ~~~, any characters

**Closing Delimiter Rules:**
- Can be indented
- Must be alone on line
- No content after ~~~ on same line

**Use Cases:**
- CUDA kernels
- Python functions
- Shader code
- SQL queries
- Prompt templates
- Documentation blocks

**Critical:**
Use ~~~ for code. Use """ for structured data.
Never mix them up.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_comments
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = syntax
  overview = "// comments are stripped everywhere except inside ~~~ verbatim blocks - enables inline documentation."
  doc = ~~~
Rio Comment Syntax:

**Comment Rules:**
```rio
// Full line comment

Project {
  project = MyProject  // trailing comment
  // comment between fields
  domain = tsu  // another trailing comment
}
```

**Behavior:**
- // starts comment
- Comment continues to end of line
- Comments stripped during parsing
- Trailing comments removed

**Exception:**
Inside ~~~ blocks, // is preserved:
```rio
Op {
  code = ~~~
// This is a C++ comment, preserved exactly
void my_function() {
    // This too
}
~~~
}
```

**Best Practices:**
- Document block purposes
- Explain non-obvious values
- Mark TODOs and FIXMEs
- Provide context for future readers

Comments make Rio files self-documenting.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = rio_file_organization
  category = rio_format
  topic_type = best_practice
  overview = "Organize Rio files by domain, then hierarchy: Domain/Project/Hardware first, then Models, then Operations, then supporting components."
  doc = ~~~
Rio File Structure Best Practices:

**Recommended Order:**
```rio
// 1. Domain definitions
Domain { name = tsu }

// 2. Hardware specifications
Hardware { hardware = tsu_v1 }
Hardware { hardware = gpu_a100 }

// 3. Project declarations
Project { 
  project = MyOptimizer
  domain = tsu
  hardware = tsu_v1
}

// 4. Models
Model { model = main_model }

// 5. Layers (hierarchical)
